Fix two distinct kinetic-fling defects, both device-measured.

BUG 1 - the fling dies outright on busy pages ("scrolling stops dead").

A gesture's end (touch-up) event carries a zero delta. On a busy page the main
thread stalls and that synthesized end event lands hundreds of ms late, so
pruning the scroll history against its timestamp (a hardcoded 200ms window)
discards every motion sample that preceded it. What remains is a single
zero-delta event: one sample, zero span, zero accumulated delta, and therefore
zero velocity. computeVelocity() returns {0,0} and the fling never starts - the
page stops dead under the finger rather than coasting weakly.

  Measured with WEBKIT_FLINGVEL_LOG=1, identical scripted flick:
    franceinfo.fr          3 of 8 flicks -> velocity 0,0
    trivial local page     0 of 5 flicks
  When the fling does start, franceinfo is completely normal (coast 1038-1227px,
  actual/predicted ratio 0.97-0.99) - identical to the local page. So momentum on
  heavy sites is not weak, it is binary: full or absent. Alternating between the
  two unpredictably is what makes scrolling feel unharmonized, and it is why
  tuning WEBKIT_KINETIC_DECEL_FRICTION never evened it out - friction cannot
  scale a velocity of zero.

  Fix: only events that carry motion age out the history, and the velocity span
  is measured across motion samples only, so a late zero-delta end event can
  neither prune the samples nor stretch the denominator.
  WEBKIT_KINETIC_END_EVENT_FIX=0 restores upstream behaviour.

BUG 2 - repeated flicks run away ("scrolls way too fast", e.g. YouTube).

accumulateVelocity() adds a multiple of the previous fling's velocity when a new
flick lands in the same direction, and the sum becomes the *next* flick's
previousVelocity. Upstream caps the multiplier at 6, so a single stacked flick
can already reach 7x, and feed-scrolling compounds it geometrically into a
scroll far faster than the finger ever asked for. The constant is desktop
trackpad tuning; touch flick-stacking is a different regime.

  Fix: default the accumulation cap to 1.0 (a deliberate second flick still adds
  about one previous fling's worth - the familiar "flick again to go faster" -
  without the blow-up) and bound the accumulated result absolutely.
  WEBKIT_KINETIC_VELOCITY_ACCUM_MAX (0..6, 6 = upstream) and
  WEBKIT_KINETIC_MAX_VELOCITY (px/s, 0 = no clamp) tune both.

Also corrects a systematic bias in the same estimator: N samples contribute N
deltas, but (last - first) spans only the N-1 intervals between them, inflating
every fling by N/(N-1) - device-measured at a consistent 1.09-1.12x. Each delta
is now charged one mean interval, making the estimate sample-count independent.
WEBKIT_KINETIC_VELOCITY_BIAS_FIX=0 restores the upstream arithmetic.

Note this leaves the reduced tick rate on franceinfo (~70/s vs ~120/s on an idle
page) untouched: those flings coast the correct distance but are rendered less
often, which is a paint-path issue, not a momentum one.

Must apply AFTER the two webkit-flingvel-*-diagnostic.patch files (shares
flingVelLogEnabled() and their computeVelocity context).

